\ Bounded-memory regression for `with-timeout`.
\
\ with-timeout builds a nested scope with two tasks per call: the main task
\ running the quotation and a timer task. It now reaps both when the scope
\ exits, so a program that calls with-timeout in a loop stays bounded no matter
\ how many times it runs. Each call's main task returns instantly and wins the
\ race, so the timer is cancelled. Before the reap fix, every completed main and
\ timer task lingered in the scheduler's finished list until teardown, leaking
\ about half a megabyte per call, and this loop exhausted the cap in the .flags
\ sidecar partway through and died with a memory-limit error instead of printing
\ the marker.
\
\ The same missing accounting also never counted these two tasks in their
\ worker's active-task counter, which the completion path decrements, so the
\ counter underflowed. The reap and the matching spawn-side increment are one
\ fix; this test guards the memory half.

use "time" ;

[
  500 [
    [ 42 ] 5 seconds with-timeout drop
  ] times
] task-scope

"bounded" print-line
